{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This is the statute of the Torah</b> – Because the Satan and the nations of the world taunt Israel, saying: 'What is this commandment?' Therefore Scripture wrote concerning it 'statute' (חוקה) — it is a decree from before Me, and you have no permission to question it. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 19:2) from the words of our Sages (Yoma 67b). I have already written concerning the matter of the goat sent away (Leviticus 16:8) what reason the nations have to taunt us in this matter more than in the case of the other offerings that atone, some of which effect purification like the offerings of the zav and the woman who gives birth: it is because it is performed outside [the Sanctuary], it appears to them that it is sacrificed to the goat-demons in the open field. But the truth is that its purpose is to remove the spirit of impurity, and its burning is like a pleasing fragrance outside. And the reason for the impurity of the corpse traces back to the venom of the serpent, for those who depart [this life] by a kiss [i.e., the righteous who die by divine kiss] should not logically defile, which is what they said: 'The righteous do not defile.' Therefore Scripture said, 'This is the statute of the Torah,' meaning that which is engraved from the Torah, and it is the Oral Torah. Accordingly it is a cow, and it is red, as a measure of justice, and it was given to Elazar to be performed before him even by the hand of a non-priest (zar), but the deputy [High Priest] must oversee its performance so that it be done with his intent, lest they harbor an evil thought concerning it like the nations and the Satan. This passage completes the Torah of the priests, and it was written here after the gifts of the priesthood, to say that even the purification of Israel shall be through a priest. And the sense of 'This is the statute of the Torah which YHWH has commanded' is like the sense of 'And to Moses He said: Go up to YHWH' (Exodus 24:1), or it is as though inverted (מסורס): 'Speak to the children of Israel: This is the statute of the Torah which YHWH has commanded, saying:'" "<b>Speak</b> and not 'they shall speak' (ודברו), for Moses is the principal." "And the meaning of <b>to you</b> is that they shall do so in the wilderness at that time, and afterward (Numbers 19:10) He commands that it be for the children of Israel and for the stranger as an everlasting statute, that they shall do so for their generations. Similarly, 'And you shall take to you pure olive oil' etc. (Exodus 27:20), and afterward 'an everlasting statute throughout their generations' (ibid. v. 21). And He said <b>and you shall give</b> (ונתתם) and not 'and you shall give' (ונתת), to include Aaron with him for his honor, for both of them shall command Elazar to slaughter it before him. Or He said <b>and you shall give</b> (ונתתם) to the many — now and for generations. And the reason is that Elazar who slaughters takes it from the hand of the court, for they give it to him so that it be performed with their authorization and on their behalf, as we learned (Parah 3:5): 'And who made them [the red heifers]? The first one Moses our Teacher made, and the second one Ezra made. Seven from Ezra onward. And who made them? Simeon the Righteous and John the High Priest made two, two, etc.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "Ramban's phrase 'במדת הדין' is rendered 'as a measure of justice.' The reference to death by a 'kiss' alludes to the rabbinic concept of מיתה בנשיקה. The closing suggestion of an inverted reading (מסורס) is translated hyper-literally.", 2 "The closing paraphrase of Mishnah Parah 3:5 is translated hyper-literally following the Hebrew as it stands in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:06.403270802Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}